@page "/about"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>About | BloodLink</PageTitle>
<PublicSite>
<main id="main-content" class="public-about">
    <header class="bl-container public-editorial-intro"><span class="public-kicker">Why BloodLink exists</span><h1>Built to make blood coordination clearer.</h1><p class="public-lead">Blood supply depends on people working together. Their information should work together, too.</p></header>
    <section class="bl-container public-about-story" aria-labelledby="story-title">
        <figure><img src="images/bloodlink-team.webp" width="1536" height="1024" fetchpriority="high" alt="Three Ghanaian healthcare professionals reviewing inventory records together in a bright hospital administration room" /><figcaption>An illustration of the teamwork behind facility coordination.</figcaption></figure>
        <div><span class="public-kicker">A shared starting point</span><h2 id="story-title">Coordination starts with the people doing the work.</h2><p>Facility staff maintain stock and record needs. Administrators review those needs and coordinate with other facilities. Each decision needs clear information and a visible next step.</p><p>BloodLink brings that work into a shared facility workspace, connecting inventory, requests and the records that explain what happened.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="public-problem" aria-labelledby="problem-title"><div class="bl-container public-story-grid"><div><span class="public-kicker">The coordination gap</span><h2 id="problem-title">Information in pieces.<br />Decisions under pressure.</h2><p>When stock records, conversations and request updates are separate, teams have to reconstruct the whole picture before they can act.</p></div><dl class="public-problem-list"><div><dt>Stock visibility</dt><dd>Knowing a quantity is only useful when the team can also see what is available.</dd></div><div><dt>Manual communication</dt><dd>Separate messages can make the latest request status difficult to follow.</dd></div><div><dt>Delayed coordination</dt><dd>Disconnected information adds work between a need and the next decision.</dd></div><div><dt>Traceability</dt><dd>Teams need a record of changes and handovers after the conversation ends.</dd></div></dl></div></section>
    <section class="bl-container public-section public-approach" aria-labelledby="approach-title"><span class="public-kicker">The BloodLink approach</span><div><h2 id="approach-title">Connect the work.<br />Keep responsibility clear.</h2><div><p class="public-lead">One workflow, with a role for each member of the team.</p><p>Staff record needs. Facility administrators review, search and coordinate requests. System administrators oversee facility access. Inventory and activity records give that work a shared context.</p><a class="public-text-link" href="/how-it-works">Follow the workflow <AppIcon Name="arrow-right" Size="18" /></a></div></div></section>
    <section class="public-principles" aria-labelledby="principles-title"><div class="bl-container"><span class="public-kicker">Design principles</span><h2 id="principles-title">What we put first.</h2><div class="public-principle-grid"><article><span>01</span><h3>Clarity</h3><p>Make stock, statuses and next steps easy to understand.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Accountability</h3><p>Keep a visible record of changes and coordination.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Speed</h3><p>Reduce the effort of finding the information a decision needs.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Facility control</h3><p>Keep responsibilities with authorised facility teams.</p></article></div></div></section>
    <section class="public-final-cta bl-container"><h2>See the shared workflow in action.</h2><a class="bl-btn bl-btn-primary bl-btn-lg" href="/how-it-works">How BloodLink works <AppIcon Name="arrow-right" Size="18" /></a></section>
</main>
</PublicSite>
